Z={
1:"Commentators note that the line of teachers runs Kṛṣṇa, Vivasvān, Manu, Ikṣvāku: from the divine to the sun, to the first lawgiver, to the first king. Madhusūdana says this shows the teaching is meant for rulers and householders. Kālidāsa’s poem Raghuvaṃśa celebrates the kings of this solar line, praising them for ruling with self-control.",
2:"Commentators note mahatā kālena, “by long time”: even true teachings can fade over generations. Many reform movements in Indian history, from the Bhakti saints to modern teachers, saw their work as restoring a lost understanding. The Gītā itself presents Kṛṣṇa as doing this.",
3:"Commentators note purātanaḥ, “ancient”: the teaching is old, yet given anew. Madhusūdana says the Lord gives it to Arjuna because of his devotion and friendship. Many teachers say spiritual knowledge is passed on through relationship, not only through books.",
4:"Commentators note that Arjuna’s question is about time: how can one born recently have taught in ancient times? Madhusūdana says Arjuna knows Kṛṣṇa is divine but wants it explained for others. The question opens the Gītā’s teaching on divine incarnation.",
5:"Commentators note parantapa, “scorcher of foes”: Kṛṣṇa reminds Arjuna of his strength even as he points to his ignorance. Many Indian traditions teach that we have lived many lives; children who seem to remember past lives are a subject of folk tales and some modern research.",
6:"Commentators note prakṛtiṃ svām adhiṣṭhāya, “governing my own nature”: the Lord is master of nature, not its servant. Madhusūdana says the Lord’s birth is like a magician’s appearance: real to the viewers, but the magician is not bound by it. Rāmānuja insists the Lord’s body is real and divine.",
7:"Commentators note that the verse is often recited at times of social crisis as a promise that things will be set right. Many reformers and saints in Indian history have been seen by their followers as partial manifestations of the Divine. The verse is also inscribed in many temples and public buildings.",
8:"Commentators note yuge yuge, “age after age”: the Lord’s coming is not once but repeated. Madhusūdana says each age has its own form of help. Some modern teachers read the verse more broadly: whenever good people stand up for justice, the divine purpose is at work through them.",
9:"Commentators note tattvataḥ, “truly”: knowing the avatāra in truth, not just as a story. Madhusūdana says one who truly knows the Lord’s birth knows that he is free, and that knowledge itself frees. The festivals of Janmāṣṭamī and Rāma Navamī celebrate the divine births this verse praises.",
10:"Commentators note bahavaḥ, “many”: the path is well travelled. Madhusūdana lists sages like Śuka, Vāmadeva and Janaka as examples. Vāmadeva, according to the Aitareya Upaniṣad, realised the Self while still in his mother’s womb.",
11:"Vivekananda quoted this verse in his opening address at the Parliament of the World’s Religions in Chicago in 1893, together with a hymn that says: as rivers from different sources mingle in the sea, so different paths lead to the Lord. The verse became a symbol of Hindu openness.",
12:"Commentators note kṣipram, “quickly”: rewards of action come fast in the human world. Madhusūdana says this is why people prefer ritual for results: it works. The Gītā does not deny this but points to a higher aim.",
13:"Commentators note that the second half of the verse is often overlooked: the Lord is the maker of the order, yet the non-doer. Madhusūdana says the Lord creates without attachment, as the Gītā asks us to act. The debate about varṇa continues, but the teaching on detached action is clear.",
14:"Commentators note that the verse gives a practical way to freedom: know how the Lord acts, and act likewise. Madhusūdana says knowing that the Lord is untouched by action frees one from the fear of action. Many find this verse comforting when facing hard duties.",
15:"Commentators note pūrvataram, “even earlier”: the path of selfless action is ancient. Madhusūdana says Arjuna should follow the example of the ancients rather than inventing a new path. Respect for tradition, rightly understood, gives stability.",
16:"Commentators note mokṣyase aśubhāt, “you will be freed from ill”: understanding action is itself freeing. Madhusūdana says confusion about action causes most moral suffering. Clear thinking about what to do is a spiritual practice.",
17:"Commentators note boddhavyam, “must be understood”, repeated three times: action, wrong action and inaction all need study. Madhusūdana says each has its own consequences. Courts of law also examine intention, circumstance and outcome, not just the act.",
18:"Commentators note kṛtsna-karma-kṛt, “one who does all actions”: the wise one is fully active. Madhusūdana says he does everything without the sense of doership. Some modern readers compare this to the state athletes and artists call flow, when action happens without self-consciousness.",
19:"Commentators note jñānāgni-dagdha-karmāṇam, “whose actions are burned by the fire of knowledge”: knowledge removes the binding power of action. Madhusūdana says even the wise act, but their actions leave no trace. The image of fire returns in 4.37.",
20:"Commentators note nirāśraya, “depending on nothing”: the free person does not lean on outer supports for happiness. Madhusūdana says such a person still acts, but freely. Many find this verse a description of the ideal mature adult: engaged, generous and inwardly free.",
21:"Commentators note yata-cittātmā, “mind and self controlled”: simplicity of possessions goes with discipline of mind. Madhusūdana says such a person does only what is needed. Many Jain and Hindu monks keep only a few items: a water pot, a cloth, a staff.",
22:"Commentators note dvandvātīta, “beyond the pairs”: heat and cold, gain and loss, praise and blame no longer disturb. Madhusūdana says contentment with what comes is the mark of freedom. Many monks live this way, accepting whatever food and shelter they receive.",
23:"Commentators note gata-saṅga, “free from attachment”, and mukta, “liberated”: the person described is already free. Madhusūdana says such a one’s actions dissolve because there is no ego to hold them. The verse leads directly to the famous 4.24.",
24:"Commentators note brahma-karma-samādhinā, “absorbed in Brahman as action”: action itself becomes meditation. Madhusūdana says one who sees Brahman in every part of the act is free. The verse is printed on the walls of many school and temple dining halls in India.",
25:"Commentators note that verses 25–30 describe twelve kinds of offering. Madhusūdana says each is valid in its place. The list shows how varied spiritual practice can be: some offer things, some offer their senses, some offer breath, some offer knowledge.",
26:"Commentators note saṃyamāgniṣu, “in the fires of restraint”: restraint itself is pictured as a fire that consumes the senses’ cravings. Madhusūdana says the student and the monk practise this kind. The householder who enjoys rightly practises the second kind.",
27:"Commentators note ātma-saṃyama-yogāgni, “the fire of the yoga of self-control”: the deepest meditation. Madhusūdana says here all activity of senses and breath is offered into stillness. This is the state described in chapter 6.",
28:"Commentators note saṃśita-vratāḥ, “of firm vows”: practitioners of strict discipline. Madhusūdana says each kind of yajña requires steady practice. Many Hindu communities maintain such practices: daily study of scripture, regular fasts, pilgrimages and charity.",
29:"Commentators note prāṇāyāma-parāyaṇāḥ, “devoted to breath control”. Madhusūdana describes the three phases of breathing. Today prāṇāyāma is practised worldwide as part of yoga, and studies suggest slow breathing can calm the nervous system.",
30:"Commentators note yajña-kṣapita-kalmaṣāḥ, “sins destroyed by sacrifice”: all these practices purify. Madhusūdana says the variety shows that the Lord accepts many paths. A person can find the offering that suits their nature and circumstances.",
31:"Commentators note kuru-sattama, “best of the Kurus”: Kṛṣṇa honours Arjuna as he speaks of the nectar of offering. Madhusūdana says those who never give cannot be happy even in this world. Many find that generosity brings a happiness that hoarding never does.",
32:"Commentators note vitatāḥ, “spread out”: the many sacrifices are spread out before the Lord like offerings on an altar. Madhusūdana says knowing they are all born of action frees one from pride in any one of them. No practice makes one better than another person.",
33:"Commentators note parisamāpyate, “culminates”: all action finds its completion in knowledge. Madhusūdana says knowledge is the goal for which all practices are means. Study and reflection are therefore honoured as the highest offering.",
34:"Commentators note that the verse describes the relationship of student and teacher, guru and śiṣya, central to Indian education. In the ancient gurukula system, students lived with the teacher, served the household and learned through daily life. The teacher was judged by both learning and life.",
35:"Commentators note na punar moham, “not again delusion”: knowledge, once gained, does not return to confusion. Madhusūdana says Arjuna will see all beings in himself and in the Lord. Śrī Vaiṣṇava teachers say this means seeing all beings as belonging to the Lord, as one’s own family.",
36:"Commentators note pāpa-kṛttamaḥ, “worst of sinners”: even the worst is not beyond hope. Madhusūdana says knowledge burns past sin as fire burns wood. Stories like Vālmīki’s, the robber who became a poet-sage, are often told with this verse.",
37:"Commentators note bhasmasāt, “to ashes”: the image is complete destruction. Madhusūdana says even the store of past karma is burned. The image of ash, vibhūti, is sacred in Śaiva practice, where it reminds devotees that all things end and only the Self remains.",
38:"Commentators note svayam, “oneself”: knowledge is discovered within, not borrowed. Madhusūdana says it comes in its own time to one prepared by yoga. Many teachers say the most important things cannot be taught, only found.",
39:"Commentators note acireṇa, “soon”: peace follows knowledge quickly. Madhusūdana says faith, dedication and self-control are the three conditions. Each supports the others: faith gives energy, dedication gives focus, self-control gives steadiness.",
40:"Commentators note that the verse names three faults: ignorance, lack of faith and chronic doubt. Madhusūdana says the doubter is worst because he cannot even begin. Teachers often say: begin with what you can trust, and let experience test it.",
41:"Commentators note ātmavantam, “self-possessed”: the one who has gathered himself. Madhusūdana says such a person is not bound by action. The verse sums up chapter 4: act with offering, see with knowledge, stay centred.",
42:"Commentators note bhārata, “descendant of Bharata”: Kṛṣṇa ends by reminding Arjuna of his royal lineage. Madhusūdana says the sword of knowledge must be taken up and used. Chapter 5 will continue the discussion of renunciation and action.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
